1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,574
Today is October 31, 2026.

3
00:00:07,574 --> 00:00:14,902
Overnight, the S and P, Nasdaq, and Dow plunged as rising Treasury yields clouded the Fed rate path.

4
00:00:14,902 --> 00:00:20,834
Wall Street suffered its fiercest October deleveraging shock in over a decade to close out the week.

5
00:00:20,834 --> 00:00:29,020
The Nasdaq One Hundred plummeted 3.46 percent on Friday, compounding Thursday's 1.38 percent slide.

6
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:38,260
The Nasdaq Composite dropped 2.86 percent, shedding 747 points and leaving a massive 380-point downward gap.

7
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:45,552
The S and P 500 fell 1.82 percent on Friday, logging its third consecutive weekly loss.

8
00:00:45,552 --> 00:00:53,187
The Dow Jones Industrial Average retreated 1.09 percent, cushioned slightly by defensive value rotation.

9
00:00:53,187 --> 00:01:01,814
Semiconductors led the selloff as the Philly Semiconductor Index collapsed 7.2 percent to close at 10,688.

10
00:01:01,814 --> 00:01:09,179
Intraday chip losses briefly hit 9.3 percent, triggering a systemic market circuit breaker in South Korea.

11
00:01:09,179 --> 00:01:17,917
Foreign institutional funds dumped SK Hynix and Samsung Electronics relentlessly, overwhelming domestic Korean retail dip buyers.

12
00:01:17,917 --> 00:01:24,143
Behind this equity rout lies a toxic macro mix of sticky inflation and hawkish central banks.

13
00:01:24,143 --> 00:01:32,905
Third-quarter US GDP expanded at a 3.4 percent annualized pace, beating consensus estimates of 3.1 percent.

14
00:01:32,905 --> 00:01:41,079
Business investment surged 12.7 percent on artificial intelligence spending, proving aggregate demand remains running hot.

15
00:01:41,079 --> 00:01:49,682
Meanwhile, headline PCE inflation rose 0.5 percent month over month, reaching 3.6 percent year over year.

16
00:01:49,682 --> 00:01:58,150
Core PCE climbed 0.3 percent monthly and 3.1 percent annually, staying stubbornly above the two percent target.

17
00:01:58,150 --> 00:02:04,817
Overheating growth and rebounding energy prices are actively compressing room for Federal Reserve rate cuts.

18
00:02:04,817 --> 00:02:09,804
Global central banks amplified this rate shock across sovereign debt markets.

19
00:02:09,804 --> 00:02:17,905
The Bank of Canada delivered a surprise 25-basis-point rate hike to 2.5 percent, signaling potential further tightening.

20
00:02:17,905 --> 00:02:24,780
The European Central Bank held rates at 2.5 percent but issued hawkish warnings on persistent inflation.

21
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:31,214
At home, Fed Chair Warsh announced plans to reduce annual policy meetings from eight down to six.

22
00:02:31,214 --> 00:02:38,603
Fewer decision windows and cautious forward guidance pushed long-end Treasury yields higher, steepening the yield curve.

23
00:02:38,603 --> 00:02:45,123
Under the surface, institutional positioning and options mechanics severely distorted market volatility.

24
00:02:45,123 --> 00:02:51,140
Earlier this week, the VIX fell for four straight sessions despite heavy technology selling.

25
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:59,474
Heavy pre-event hedging and explosive zero-day-to-expiry options trading temporarily suppressed traditional 30-day implied volatility.

26
00:02:59,474 --> 00:03:06,312
On Friday, that coiled spring snapped as the VIX surged to 24 intraday before fading twenty percent.

27
00:03:06,312 --> 00:03:12,145
Goldman Sachs data shows hedge fund gross leverage is soaring while net leverage drops sharply.

28
00:03:12,145 --> 00:03:19,976
Institutions pivoted from shorting single stocks to aggressively shorting broad index ETFs, reaching ten-year extremes.

29
00:03:19,976 --> 00:03:28,371
Short interest concentrated heavily in Triple Q, trapping Monday's 1.6-billion-dollar big-tech call buyers in deep losses.

30
00:03:28,371 --> 00:03:34,253
Cross-asset liquidity strain also emerged as gold and silver tumbled alongside equities.

31
00:03:34,253 --> 00:03:40,981
Forced margin calls pushed leveraged traders to liquidate precious metals and winners to raise immediate cash.

32
00:03:40,981 --> 00:03:48,947
Bank of America's Bull and Bear Indicator edged down from 7.2 to a neutral 7.1 on widening credit spreads.

33
00:03:49,064 --> 00:03:55,104
Geopolitical and regulatory tail risks are compounding macro uncertainty ahead of November.

34
00:03:55,054 --> 00:04:02,211
Betting markets price a 93.7 percent probability Democrats take the House and 70 percent for the Senate.

35
00:04:02,211 --> 00:04:09,282
A divided government threatens tax cuts and deregulation while raising AI energy rules and debt-ceiling gridlock.

36
00:04:09,282 --> 00:04:15,912
In the Middle East, US Central Command briefed leadership on fresh military strike options against Iran.

37
00:04:15,912 --> 00:04:22,076
Rising crude oil prices reflect growing consensus that kinetic conflict could resume in November.

38
00:04:22,076 --> 00:04:28,927
Meanwhile, crypto markets reeled after hackers stole a record 6.3 billion dollars from Binance.

39
00:04:28,927 --> 00:04:36,525
Roughly 3 billion dollars in stablecoins were frozen, while 3.3 billion in Bitcoin and Ether remain tracked.

40
00:04:36,525 --> 00:04:45,201
Binance pledged full reimbursement from its 6.34-billion-dollar reserve, yet Circle and Coinbase still sold off sharply.

41
00:04:45,201 --> 00:04:54,368
Technically, the S and P 500 broke its 7,600 support, touching 7,527 before a late bounce.

42
00:04:54,368 --> 00:05:03,951
Bulls must reclaim 7,600 early next week to avoid testing 7,500 or the 7,300 JPMorgan collar floor.

43
00:05:03,951 --> 00:05:12,566
Triple Q gapped below 700, pointing toward the 680 Fibonacci retracement and the 660 extreme support zone.

44
00:05:12,566 --> 00:05:18,351
The Philly Semiconductor Index is testing triple support at the critical 10,000 threshold.

45
00:05:18,351 --> 00:05:26,145
Losing 10,000 risks a devastating C-wave drop to 8,300, which would erase all year-to-date chip gains.

46
00:05:26,145 --> 00:05:32,138
This macro tightening has triggered a foundational valuation reset across the technology sector.

47
00:05:32,138 --> 00:05:38,608
Investors no longer reward raw artificial intelligence revenue growth or distant narrative promises.

48
00:05:38,608 --> 00:05:45,164
Wall Street is now ruthlessly auditing whether massive capital expenditures generate real free cash flow.

49
00:05:45,164 --> 00:05:52,554
Hyperscaler capital spending is approaching one trillion dollars annually, exhausting internal corporate cash buffers.

50
00:05:52,554 --> 00:06:00,348
Three of the four largest cloud giants, namely Google, Amazon, and Meta, now post negative free cash flow.

51
00:06:00,348 --> 00:06:07,039
Tech titans have morphed from self-funded cash cows into debt-financed builders fighting an expensive arms race.

52
00:06:07,039 --> 00:06:13,669
Microsoft stunned bond markets by launching its first investment-grade debt offering since 2017.

53
00:06:13,669 --> 00:06:20,937
Despite holding nearly 100 billion dollars in cash, Microsoft is borrowing at least 25 billion dollars.

54
00:06:20,937 --> 00:06:28,547
Those two-to-forty-year bonds yield over 6 percent, siphoning institutional credit away from smaller growth enterprises.

55
00:06:28,547 --> 00:06:36,941
Credit default swaps on Amazon, Intel, Broadcom, and AMD spiked to record highs on return-on-investment fears.

56
00:06:37,064 --> 00:06:44,023
Capital is fleeing debt-heavy AI builders and software while rotating into defensive cash-flow sanctuaries.

57
00:06:43,973 --> 00:06:51,449
The software ETF plunged over 3.5 percent after Google unveiled its frontier Gemini 4 Carbon model.

58
00:06:51,449 --> 00:06:59,010
Gemini 4 Carbon's advanced coding and vulnerability detection sparked fears that AI will replace enterprise software.

59
00:06:59,010 --> 00:07:06,914
ServiceNow crashed 27 percent after cutting fourth-quarter contract obligations growth guidance to 18.5 percent.

60
00:07:06,914 --> 00:07:15,775
Debt interest from its 7.75-billion-dollar Armis acquisition also dragged ServiceNow's GAAP net income down 45 percent.

61
00:07:15,775 --> 00:07:23,581
Fortinet plunged 25 percent as firewall hardware growth decelerated from 52 percent down to 37 percent.

62
00:07:23,581 --> 00:07:31,008
Fortinet also trimmed full-year service revenue guidance, shaking confidence in recurring cybersecurity cash flows.

63
00:07:31,008 --> 00:07:38,434
Reddit collapsed nearly 30 percent to 91 dollars as Google's AI Overviews choked off search referral traffic.

64
00:07:38,434 --> 00:07:45,223
In memory chips, investors punished second-order growth deceleration despite record third-quarter profits.

65
00:07:45,223 --> 00:07:52,932
Samsung Electronics topped 10 trillion won in operating profit, yet chip earnings missed lofty street expectations.

66
00:07:52,932 --> 00:08:00,334
Kioxia signaled slowing fourth-quarter momentum, while SanDisk fell 8 percent despite a 70-fold profit surge.

67
00:08:00,334 --> 00:08:08,986
SanDisk posted 85 percent gross margins, a 5-billion-dollar buyback, and an eight-for-one stock split for November 19.

68
00:08:08,986 --> 00:08:16,976
Meanwhile, Marvell Technology fell on dilution fears amid talks to acquire optical rival Lumentum for ten billion dollars.

69
00:08:16,976 --> 00:08:23,937
Exiting tech money poured into healthcare and energy, lifting ExxonMobil, Chevron, and Eli Lilly.

70
00:08:23,937 --> 00:08:33,483
Eli Lilly jumped 6.63 percent to record highs after third-quarter revenue surged 39 percent to 24.47 billion dollars.

71
00:08:33,483 --> 00:08:41,118
Lilly raised full-year revenue guidance to 92 billion dollars, commanding 63 percent of the weight-loss drug market.

72
00:08:41,118 --> 00:08:49,120
Conversely, Caterpillar fell despite an earnings beat as soft fourth-quarter sales guidance signaled cooling global industrial capex.

73
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:55,125
In our individual stock deep dive, Google stands at the epicenter of the valuation reset.

74
00:08:55,125 --> 00:09:03,017
Google suffered its worst single-day drop in history on Thursday, plunging 12.64 percent to 280 dollars.

75
00:09:03,017 --> 00:09:09,169
Shares slipped another 1.4 percent on Friday with zero institutional dip-buying support.

76
00:09:09,169 --> 00:09:17,184
Third-quarter revenue and earnings both missed expectations, while free cash flow turned negative at 9.1 billion dollars.

77
00:09:17,184 --> 00:09:25,775
Search advertising revenue grew 12 percent to 63.35 billion dollars, decelerating from 19 percent in quarter one.

78
00:09:25,775 --> 00:09:32,478
Bears argue that AI Overviews and automated agents are cannibalizing high-margin paid search clicks.

79
00:09:32,478 --> 00:09:40,664
However, bulls note that full-day turnover was just 0.84 percent, showing 99 percent of holders stayed locked.

80
00:09:40,664 --> 00:09:47,907
Google Cloud revenue surged 108 percent past 30 billion dollars, rapidly closing the gap with search.

81
00:09:47,907 --> 00:09:55,615
On a sum-of-the-parts basis, Gemini alone rivals Anthropic's two-trillion-dollar valuation across one billion users.

82
00:09:55,615 --> 00:10:03,691
At a 3.5-trillion-dollar market cap and under 15 times trailing earnings, core assets trade at a steep discount.

83
00:10:03,691 --> 00:10:10,125
Berkshire Hathaway holds a major stake after investing 10 billion dollars earlier in 2026.

84
00:10:10,125 --> 00:10:17,944
Berkshire's mid-November 13F filing will be a pivotal catalyst, as Greg Abel holds 300 billion dollars in cash.

85
00:10:17,944 --> 00:10:25,419
Technically, expect a weak bounce near 280 dollars before a retest of March lows at 272.

86
00:10:25,419 --> 00:10:32,907
Long-term investors can scale in near 280 dollars and reserve heavy ammunition for major support at 250.

87
00:10:33,032 --> 00:10:41,364
Amazon presents a contrasting hyperscaler battleground after plunging post-earnings before recovering to close Friday down 1.4 percent.

88
00:10:41,314 --> 00:10:50,088
Third-quarter revenue rose 12 percent to 201.9 billion dollars, missing the 202.3 billion consensus estimate.

89
00:10:50,088 --> 00:10:57,662
Earnings of 1.89 dollars per share missed expectations of 1.96 as interest expenses climbed.

90
00:10:57,662 --> 00:11:05,505
Net income slipped 2 percent against tough comparisons from last year's 9.5-billion-dollar Anthropic investment gain.

91
00:11:05,505 --> 00:11:14,071
On the bright side, AWS cloud revenue accelerated 39.3 percent, marking its fastest growth in five years.

92
00:11:14,071 --> 00:11:22,895
AWS backlog reached nearly 578 billion dollars, with management projecting over 40 percent growth through 2028.

93
00:11:22,895 --> 00:11:31,498
Yet full-year capital expenditures of 220 billion dollars pushed third-quarter free cash flow to negative 33.5 billion.

94
00:11:31,498 --> 00:11:39,378
Trailing twelve-month free cash flow hit negative 42.4 billion dollars, forcing Amazon toward debt financing.

95
00:11:39,378 --> 00:11:47,025
Unlike Google, Amazon found buyers on Friday because its core retail moat faces less existential AI disruption.

96
00:11:47,025 --> 00:11:54,598
Amazon also carries far less valuation froth, up just 4 percent this year after gaining 5 percent last year.

97
00:11:54,598 --> 00:12:02,723
Key technical support sits at the July lows between 225 and 226 dollars for tactical rebound trades.

98
00:12:02,723 --> 00:12:09,843
In semiconductors, Intel suffered a historic collapse, plunging up to 31 percent intraday on Friday.

99
00:12:09,843 --> 00:12:17,858
Third-quarter revenue rose 21 percent to 16.5 billion dollars with adjusted earnings of 52 cents per share.

100
00:12:17,858 --> 00:12:25,824
However, fourth-quarter revenue guidance of 15 to 16 billion dollars badly missed the 17-billion-dollar consensus.

101
00:12:25,824 --> 00:12:33,483
Adjusted gross margin guidance imploded to 38 percent, far below the 42.8 percent street expectation.

102
00:12:33,483 --> 00:12:41,387
Client computing revenue fell 5 percent as rising memory costs choked personal computer demand through early 2027.

103
00:12:41,387 --> 00:12:48,483
An unexpected Israeli fab explosion and lagging foundry commercialization further shattered the turnaround thesis.

104
00:12:48,483 --> 00:12:54,770
Wall Street also shorted Intel ahead of Tuesday's midterms to price in Democratic victory risks.

105
00:12:54,770 --> 00:13:02,968
The US government's 433.3-million-share stake lost over 11 billion dollars in paper value in a single day.

106
00:13:02,968 --> 00:13:10,971
Having gapped below its 250-day moving average and April gap, Intel's 65-dollar level is no hard floor.

107
00:13:10,971 --> 00:13:18,054
Avoid catching this falling knife as shares risk sliding toward 55 dollars during year-end tax-loss harvesting.

108
00:13:18,054 --> 00:13:24,831
Looking ahead to next week's catalyst calendar, Monday after-hours brings crucial earnings from Apple and Palantir.

109
00:13:24,831 --> 00:13:32,539
Apple reclaimed the world's top market cap at 5 trillion dollars, overtaking Nvidia at 4.93 trillion.

110
00:13:32,539 --> 00:13:39,659
By licensing Google's AI models instead of burning cash on data centers, Apple has become a defensive haven.

111
00:13:39,659 --> 00:13:47,858
Wall Street expects Apple revenue of 113.3 billion dollars and earnings of 1.99 dollars per share.

112
00:13:47,858 --> 00:13:54,721
Holiday fourth-quarter guidance will determine whether Apple can single-handedly stabilize the broader technology sector.

113
00:13:54,721 --> 00:14:02,809
Palantir is projected to post 85 percent revenue growth, with commercial revenue doubling past 1.1 billion dollars.

114
00:14:02,809 --> 00:14:10,603
With implied post-earnings moves exceeding 11 percent, chasing high-valuation momentum before the print carries extreme risk.

115
00:14:10,603 --> 00:14:18,017
Tuesday features the US midterm elections alongside earnings from AMD, Super Micro, and Shopify.

116
00:14:18,017 --> 00:14:25,321
AMD broke below its 120-day moving average and must reclaim it immediately to avoid technical breakdown.

117
00:14:25,321 --> 00:14:32,454
Wednesday brings McDonald's and Qualcomm, while full tech stabilization awaits Nvidia's mid-November earnings report.

118
00:14:32,454 --> 00:14:39,427
With November corporate buyback windows reopening and hedge fund shorts stretched, avoid panic shorting at the bottom.

119
00:14:39,427 --> 00:14:47,110
Scale into broad index ETFs like Triple Q near 680 to 660 in four disciplined tranches.

120
00:14:47,110 --> 00:14:54,488
Keep leverage at zero, trim cash-burning small caps, and monitor credit spreads and long-end yields closely.

121
00:14:54,488 --> 00:14:58,495
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

122
00:14:58,495 --> 00:15:02,662
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
